WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
♪ [intro music] ♪

00:00:05.352 --> 00:00:06.480
Give me one minute,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.172
and I'll show you how VPC Service Controls

00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:12.894
can help you create a custom fence 
around your cloud infrastructure.

00:00:13.494 --> 00:00:15.067
Think of a cell in your body

00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:18.781
that has a context-aware control center
within the nucleus

00:00:19.194 --> 00:00:23.211
that controls what flows
in the cell membrane and what stays out.

00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:27.381
VPC Service Controls can do the same
for your cloud projects.

00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:29.390
They help you keep unwanted things

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.077
from passing through your membrane

00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:33.940
so that your data isn't retrieved
from your server,

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.661
copied, or transferred
without authorization.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:41.288
They also allow for a flexible variety
of project configurations,

00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:44.393
including hybrid environments
with on-premise services,

00:00:44.735 --> 00:00:46.384
using Private Google Access

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:50.397
or multiple cloud perimeters
that access each other via ingress

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:53.878
and egress rules
called a Secure Data Exchange.

00:00:54.563 --> 00:00:57.158
You also get near real-time security logs

00:00:57.591 --> 00:01:00.674
that report denials and activity
deemed potentially malicious,

00:01:00.956 --> 00:01:02.876
as well as IP address data.

00:01:02.876 --> 00:01:05.646
You can build that into a dashboard,
using Data Studio,

00:01:05.646 --> 00:01:07.297
since logs go to BigQuery.

00:01:07.766 --> 00:01:10.451
You can set up these rules,
either in your console

00:01:10.451 --> 00:01:13.944
or via the gcloud terminal,
or via code, using the API.

00:01:14.659 --> 00:01:17.247
There are two types
of configurations at this time.

00:01:17.247 --> 00:01:18.832
One is called enforce mode,

00:01:18.832 --> 00:01:22.125
which actively prevents access
to protected services.

00:01:22.402 --> 00:01:25.416
The other is dry run mode,
which log violations

00:01:25.416 --> 00:01:27.536
but does not prevent them from occurring.

00:01:27.766 --> 00:01:31.047
There's no additional charge
for using VPC Service Controls.

00:01:31.047 --> 00:01:33.262
Common use cases are monitoring threats,

00:01:33.262 --> 00:01:35.041
transferring data from another cloud,

00:01:35.041 --> 00:01:38.797
or to set up a Private Access
to Google APIs and services

00:01:39.023 --> 00:01:40.161
within your projects.

00:01:40.161 --> 00:01:41.976
So, next time you're looking to manage

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:45.627
your network and security perimeters
to keep your data safe,

00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:49.012
take a minute to look
at the context-aware VPC Service Controls.

00:01:49.398 --> 00:01:55.123
To learn more, check out
<i>cloud.google.com/vpc-service-controls</i>

00:01:55.743 --> 00:01:57.258
♪ [outro music] ♪

